    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The majority of the air leakage was coming from the wall by the stairs. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Main floor walls do not have sufficient insulation value for what is needed for winters in Dawson City. If you were to insulate these walls, you should add a minimum of R-20. This will also reduce the amount of air leakage in the home substancially.</mainWalls>
                <ceilingsCathedral>Insulate the sloped part of the ceiling as there is currently a very small amount of insulation. Spray foam would be your best bet unless you wanted to insulate from the exterior. </ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>The underside of the house needs to be repaired. I would recommend adding at least R-20 to the current insulation that it present.</foundation>
                <windows>The single pane windows on the main floor should all be replaced. The rest of the double paned windows should be as well but they are not priority.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If you decide to renovate the house and reduce the air leakage. It would be  good idea to look into having an HRV installed. This will provide proper ventialtion to the home, This can improve the quality of air in the home and help prevent mold and mildew growth in the home.</VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
